        <h2>Due Diligence Fee vs. Earnest Money Deposit: Strategic Distinctions</h2>
    <p>North Carolina's standard Form 2-T contract is unique among US states because it separates buyer deposits into two distinct financial mechanisms:</p>
    <div class="table-responsive">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Deposit Type</th><th>Recipient / Holding Entity</th><th>Refundable Upon Termination?</th><th>Credited at Closing?</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Due Diligence Fee (DDF)</strong></td><td>Delivered directly to <strong>Seller</strong></td><td><strong>Non-refundable</strong> (except for seller breach)</td><td>Yes &ndash; 100% credited against purchase price</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Earnest Money Deposit (EMD)</strong></td><td>Held in escrow by <strong>Closing Attorney</strong></td><td><strong>Refundable</strong> if terminated before 5:00 PM on Due Diligence date</td><td>Yes &ndash; 100% credited against purchase price</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h4>How much are closing costs in North Carolina?</h4>
      <p>In North Carolina, buyer closing costs typically range from 2.0% to 3.5% of the purchase price when financing. Seller closing costs range from 6.0% to 8.0%, driven by real estate broker commissions (5% to 6%), NC excise stamp taxes ($2 per $1,000), and attorney document preparation fees.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h4>What is the North Carolina Real Estate Excise Stamp Tax rate?</h4>
      <p>The North Carolina Excise Stamp Tax (deed transfer tax) is $1.00 for each $500 of value, or fraction thereof, equaling an effective rate of 0.20% ($2.00 per $1,000). By state statute (N.C.G.S. 105-228.30), the seller is legally responsible for paying this tax at settlement.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h4>What is the Due Diligence Fee in North Carolina?</h4>
      <p>In North Carolina standard real estate contracts (NC REALTORS Form 2-T), the Due Diligence Fee is a non-refundable payment made by the buyer directly to the seller upon contract acceptance. It grants the buyer the unilateral right to terminate the contract during the due diligence period for any reason. At closing, the fee is credited 100% toward the buyer's purchase price and closing costs.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h4>Is an attorney required to close a home sale in North Carolina?</h4>
      <p>Yes. North Carolina is an attorney closing state. Under NC General Statutes, only a licensed attorney can conduct the title search, prepare legal closing documents, and supervise settlement funds.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h4>Who pays for title insurance in North Carolina?</h4>
      <p>In North Carolina, the buyer pays for both the Lender Title Insurance Policy and the Owner Title Insurance Policy. Rates are filed with and regulated by the NC Department of Insurance.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h4>How much are closing costs on a $400,000 home in North Carolina?</h4>
      <p>On a $400,000 home with 20% down ($320,000 loan): A buyer pays ~$9,450 (including $1,550 title insurance, $900 attorney fee, $1,400 lender fees, and $5,600 tax/ins escrow). A seller pays ~$25,100 (including $20,000 commission, $800 excise stamp tax, $300 attorney doc prep, and property tax prorations).</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h4>Are there local municipal transfer taxes in North Carolina?</h4>
      <p>A few specific coastal counties (such as Dare County) impose an additional 1.0% local land transfer tax. However, in the vast majority of NC counties (including Mecklenburg, Wake, Guilford, and Durham), only the standard 0.20% state excise tax applies.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h4>How do property taxes prorate at closing in North Carolina?</h4>
      <p>NC property taxes are levied on a calendar year basis (January 1 to December 31) and become due on September 1. If closing occurs before taxes are billed, the seller gives the buyer a credit for the days they owned the home; if closing occurs after the seller paid the annual bill, the buyer reimburses the seller.</p>
    </div>

